Title: The Call of the Wild
Credit: Written by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from the novel by Jack London
Draft date: 7/30/2026
Notes: Period live-action; cold northern light; Buck a massive mixed St. Bernard–shepherd, 140 lbs, rich brown coat with white chest splash and brown muzzle markings—identity locked for the whole film

FADE IN:

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S PLACE - SANTA CLARA VALLEY - DAY

Sun-kissed lawns, poplars, a wide veranda. BUCK—broad-breasted, long-furred, regal—rules the demesne: plunges in the cement tank, escorts the Judge's daughters, carries grandsons on his back, stalks among yelping fox terriers like a king.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck did not read the newspapers, or he would have known that trouble was brewing... men had found a yellow metal in the Northland. These men wanted dogs.

INT. JUDGE MILLER'S LIBRARY - NIGHT

Buck lies at JUDGE MILLER's feet before a roaring fire. Dignity. Warmth. Civilization.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had lived the four years of his life the life of a sated aristocrat. But he did not know that Manuel, one of the gardener's helpers, was an undesirable acquaintance.

EXT. ORCHARD AND COLLEGE PARK STATION - NIGHT

MANUEL leads Buck on what seems a stroll. At the little flag station a STRANGER waits. Money chinks between them.

STRANGER
You might wrap up the goods before you deliver 'm.

Manuel doubles stout rope around Buck's neck under the collar.

MANUEL
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

Buck growls. The rope tightens. He springs; the stranger grapples his throat, throws him, chokes him senseless. They flag a train and hurl him into the baggage car.

INT. BAGGAGE CAR - NIGHT

Buck's eyes open—unbridled anger of a kidnapped king. He seizes the stranger's hand in his jaws until choked out again.

STRANGER
Yep, has fits. I'm takin' 'm up for the boss to 'Frisco.

EXT. SAN FRANCISCO WATERFRONT SHED - NIGHT

The stranger's hand is wrapped in a bloody handkerchief. SALOON-KEEPER and helpers file off Buck's brass collar and fling him into a cagelike crate. Buck storms the bars. They poke sticks. He learns, and lies sullen.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He felt oppressed by the vague sense of impending calamity.

EXT. SEATTLE HIGH-WALLED BACK YARD - DAY

Four men set the crate down. THE MAN IN THE RED SWEATER—stout, sweater sagging at the neck—signs for it, brings hatchet and club.

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Now, you red-eyed devil.

He pries the crate open. Buck launches one hundred and forty pounds of fury. The club checks him mid-air. Again and again he charges; again and again the club smashes him down. Blood from nose, mouth, ears. A final shrewd blow knocks him senseless.

MAN ON WALL
He's no slouch at dog-breakin', that's wot I say.

Buck's senses return. The man pats the head he pounded.

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Well, Buck, my boy, we've had our little ruction. Be a good dog and all 'll go well. Be a bad dog, and I'll whale the stuffin' outa you. Understand?

Buck drinks. Bolts raw meat from the man's hand.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was beaten (he knew that); but he was not broken. A man with a club was a lawgiver, a master to be obeyed. That club was his introduction to the reign of primitive law.

Other dogs arrive and pass under the same dominion. Money changes hands. A little weazened man—PERRAULT—eyes Buck.

PERRAULT
Sacredam! Dat one dam bully dog! Eh? How moch?

MAN IN RED SWEATER
Three hundred, and a present at that. Seem' it's government money, you ain't got no kick coming, eh, Perrault?

EXT. DECK OF THE NARWHAL - DAY

Seattle falls away. Buck and good-natured CURLY (a Newfoundland) stand with Perrault. Below, FRANCOIS—French-Canadian half-breed, swarthy—takes charge. Snow-white SPITZ smiles a treacherous smile. Gloomy DAVE ignores all.

Buck steps onto white mush. Springs back. Sniffs. Licks. It bites like fire and is gone. Onlookers laugh. His first snow.

EXT. DYEA BEACH - DAY

Nightmare of confusion. Primordial. Dogs and men who know no law but club and fang.

Curly makes friendly advances to a husky. A flash of teeth—her face ripped open. Thirty huskies close in a silent circle. She goes down. They bury her, screaming. Francois swings an axe; men with clubs scatter them. Curly lies limp in bloody snow.

Spitz runs out his scarlet tongue and laughs. Buck's hatred is born—bitter and deathless.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So that was the way. No fair play. Once down, that was the end of you. Well, he would see to it that he never went down.

Francois fastens a harness on Buck. Hurt dignity—but he is too wise to rebel. Dave nips his hindquarters; Spitz jerks the traces. Buck learns: ho, mush, swing wide.

FRANCOIS
T'ree vair' good dogs. Dat Buck, heem pool lak hell.

EXT. NORTHLAND TRAIL AND CAMPS - DAY AND NIGHT

Montage, tight: nine dogs in harness; Spitz lead, Buck between Dave and Sol-leks. Breaking trail up Dyea Canon, over Chilcoot, down lakes to Bennett. Pound and a half of sun-dried salmon. Hunger. Buck learns to eat fast, then to steal—secretly, cunningly.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
This first theft marked Buck as fit to survive. It marked the decay of his moral nature, a vain thing under the law of club and fang.

Instincts long dead wake. He fights with cut and slash and the quick wolf snap. On still cold nights he points his nose at a star and howls long and wolflike.

EXT. SHORE OF LAKE LE BARGE - NIGHT

Bleak camp on ice. Spitz steals Buck's warm nest. The beast in Buck roars—they tangle. Then pandemonium: starving village huskies raid the grub. Clubs. Blood. Spitz treacherously tears Buck's throat in the melee. The team flees onto the lake.

Later, wounded, they limp back. Half the grub gone.

FRANCOIS
Ah, my frien's, mebbe it mek you mad dog, dose many bites.

EXT. THIRTY MILE RIVER AND TRAIL - DAY

Exhausting toil on thin ice. Perrault breaks through; fire to dry. Buck's feet soften and bleed. Francois rubs them, cuts moccasin-tops into four little boots. Buck refuses to budge one morning without them—Perrault grins.

At the Pelly, DOLLY goes mad—heartbreaking wolf howl—and chases Buck. Francois's axe ends her. Spitz seizes the moment to rip Buck; Francois's lash gives Spitz the worst whipping yet.

PERRAULT
One devil, dat Spitz. Some dam day heem keel dat Buck.

FRANCOIS
Dat Buck two devils. Some dam fine day heem chew dat Spitz all up an' spit heem out on de snow. Sure. I know.

EXT. CAMP AND TRAIL TOWARD DAWSON - DAY AND NIGHT

Open war. Buck interferes between Spitz and shirkers—craftily, when Francois is not looking. Covert mutiny. The team goes to pieces. Francois's lash sings; small avail.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck wanted leadership because it was his nature—that nameless pride of the trail and trace which holds dogs to the last gasp.

EXT. FROZEN CREEK BED - NIGHT

Moonlight. A snowshoe rabbit. The whole team and fifty police huskies in full cry. Buck leads the pack—blood lust, joy to kill, ecstasy at the summit of life.

Spitz cuts across, breaks the rabbit's back. Buck drives into him shoulder to shoulder. To the death.

They circle in ghostly calm. Sixty dogs wait in a silent ring. Fang clashes fang. Spitz is practised; Buck streams blood. Then imagination: Buck rushes low, crunches the left foreleg—bone breaks. Then the right. Spitz on three legs. The circle tightens.

Buck springs. Shoulder meets shoulder. The dark circle becomes a dot on the moon-flooded snow as Spitz disappears.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck stood and looked on, the successful champion, the dominant primordial beast who had made his kill and found it good.

EXT. TRAIL CAMP - DAWN

Spitz missing. Buck covered with wounds. Francois tries to harness SOL-LEKS as lead. Buck drives Sol-leks back, stands in the place.

FRANCOIS
Look at dat Buck. Heem keel dat Spitz, heem t'ink to take de job.

Club in hand. Buck remembers the man in the red sweater—retreats, circles, refuses. For an hour they run him. At last Francois throws down the club. Buck trots in, laughing triumphantly, and takes the lead.

FRANCOIS
Nevaire such a dog as dat Buck! Heem worth one t'ousan' dollair, by Gar!

Record run to Skaguay. Then official orders. Francois weeps, arms around Buck. Perrault and Francois pass out of his life.

EXT. MAIL TRAIL - DAY

A SCOTCH HALF-BREED. Heavy toil, mail train. Buck enforces discipline. By the fire he dreams the short-legged hairy man of the younger world—and the eyes of beasts beyond the flames.

Dave sickens. Pride of trace: he bites through Sol-leks's traces to reclaim his place. They harness him. He pulls till he cannot. A revolver shot behind the river timber. Every dog knows.

EXT. SKAGUAY STREET - DAY

Worn out—Buck down to one hundred and fifteen pounds. Sold for a song to HAL (callow, cartridge belt) and CHARLES (watery eyes, fierce mustache). MERCEDES—Charles's wife, Hal's sister—chatters and flutters.

Overladen sled. Outside dogs added. Incompetence. Quarrels. Dog-food runs short. Dub shot. Outside dogs die. Billee axed in the traces. Mercedes rides; one hundred and twenty pounds more. Hal clubs the living skeletons onward.

EXT. JOHN THORNTON'S CAMP - MOUTH OF WHITE RIVER - DAY

Spring murmur. The wreck of a team staggers in. JOHN THORNTON whittles an axe-handle. Hal ignores warning of rotten ice.

HAL
Get up there, Buck! Mush on!

Whip, then club. Buck will not rise. He senses doom on the ice ahead. Thornton springs—hurls Hal backward.

JOHN THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

HAL
It's my dog. I'm going to Dawson.

Thornton knocks the knife away with the axe-handle, cuts Buck's traces. Hal, Charles, Mercedes, and the remaining dogs pull out onto the ice.

A quarter mile out the back end drops. Mercedes screams. The ice gives way. A yawning hole. Gone.

JOHN THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck licks his hand.

EXT. THORNTON'S CAMP - DAYS LATER

Buck heals. SKEET the setter washes his wounds. NIG the big black dog is friendly. For the first time—love, genuine passionate love. Thornton takes Buck's head roughly between his hands, shakes him, calls him love-names that sound like oaths.

JOHN THORNTON
God! you can all but speak!

Buck seizes Thornton's hand in his mouth—fierce, a caress. He follows at heels, fears Thornton will pass out of his life as the others did. Yet deep in the forest a call sounds. Love draws him back to the fire.

HANS and PETE arrive on the raft. They understand.

EXT. CLIFF CREST - DAY

Three hundred feet of naked bedrock below. A whim.

JOHN THORNTON
Jump, Buck!

Buck leaps. Thornton grapples him on the extreme edge; Hans and Pete drag them back.

PETE
It's uncanny.

JOHN THORNTON
No, it is splendid, and it is terrible, too. Do you know, it sometimes makes me afraid.

EXT. ELDORADO SALOON AND STREET - DAWSON - DAY

Boastful talk. MATTHEWSON slams a sack of gold dust on the bar.

MATTHEWSON
I've got a thousand dollars that says he can't start a thousand pounds.

Thornton has no thousand. JIM O'BRIEN lends it. Outside: sled with twenty fifty-pound sacks, runners frozen. Odds three to one against. Thornton kneels, cheek to cheek.

JOHN THORNTON
(whispers)
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

JOHN THORNTON
Now, Buck. Gee!

Buck swings right—crackling under runners.

JOHN THORNTON
Haw!

Left—snapping. Broken out.

JOHN THORNTON
Now, MUSH!

Buck throws himself forward. Muscles knot like live things. The sled lurches—half an inch, an inch, two—then steadily. One hundred yards. A roar of men. Thornton on his knees, shaking Buck, cursing him softly and lovingly.

SKOOKUM BENCH KING
I'll give you a thousand for him, sir—twelve hundred, sir.

JOHN THORNTON
Sir, you can go to hell, sir.

EXT. WILDERNESS TRAIL AND FOREST - MONTHS

With the sixteen hundred they go east after the lost mine. Straight meat, indefinite wandering. They find not the cabin but a shallow placer—gold like yellow butter. Sacks pile up like firewood.

Buck muses by the fire. The call grows. One night a long-drawn howl. He finds a lean timber wolf. After wary circles they sniff noses, run side by side. Then Buck remembers Thornton and turns back. The wild brother howls mournfully after him.

He kills a moose through days of tireless patience—dominant primordial beast. Returning, he smells calamity.

EXT. THORNTON CAMP CLEARING - DAY

Nig dead with an arrow. A sled-dog in death-struggle. Hans feathered with arrows. YEEHAT warriors dance about the wreckage of the spruce-bough lodge.

Rage usurps cunning. Buck becomes a live hurricane—tears the chief's throat, another, plunges among them so fast they shoot one another. Panic. They flee, crying Evil Spirit.

Pete dead in his blankets. Skeet at the pool's edge. Buck follows Thornton's trace into the water—from which no trace leads away.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Death he knew, and he knew John Thornton was dead. It left a great void which ached and ached, and which food could not fill.

Night. Full moon. From far away, sharp yelps—the pack. Buck waits motionless in the clearing. The boldest leaps; Buck breaks its neck. Three more. Then the whole pack. He holds them at a gravel bank until they draw back.

The long lean wild brother advances, whining. They touch noses. An old battle-scarred wolf howls. Buck howls. He runs with them, side by side with the wild brother, yelping as he runs.

EXT. OPEN SPACE AMONG TREES - DAY

Years later. A great, gloriously coated wolf—like and unlike all others—comes alone down from the timber land. Yellow stream from rotted moose-hide sacks sinks into the ground. He muses, howls once long and mournfully, and departs.

EXT. MOONLIT VALLEY - NIGHT

He runs at the head of the pack through pale moonlight or glimmering borealis, leaping gigantic above his fellows, great throat a-bellow.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He sings a song of the younger world, which is the song of the pack.

FADE OUT.

THE END
